Chapter: CameraSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Camera: one perspective divide, and the list form of it.

 THE WHOLE CHAPTER IS TWO DEFINITIONS AND TWO CONSTANTS, and every value below
 is chosen so the divide is exact -- a forward that divides the abscissa evenly
 and a height that differs from the eye by a whole number of metres. Nothing here
 needs a tolerance.

 THE EYE IS AT 1.2 AND THE SCREEN IS 600 TALL, and those are the two numbers a
 caller cannot pass in. Everything else is an argument: the focal comes from the
 lens and the view width from the frame, which is what keeps this chapter
 independent of both. Grading a point at exactly eye height is what pins the
 subtraction -- a chapter that read the eye as 0 puts the horizon at the top of
 the screen and every other test still passes.

 THE SIGNS ARE OPPOSITE AND THAT IS THE ASSERTION. Screen x grows with `right`
 and screen y SHRINKS with height, because the screen counts down from the top
 while the world counts up from the ground. Both directions are graded.

 We say:

Section: The Divide

 A focal of 800 and a 960-wide view, so the centre line is 480 and a point one
 forward-unit to the right lands a focal away from it.

  at : Real, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  at (right) (forward) (height) =
    project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = height }) 800.0 960.0

  x-got : List Real
  x-got =
    [ (at 0.0 10.0 1.2).x,
      (at 10.0 10.0 1.2).x,
      (at (0.0 - 5.0) 10.0 1.2).x,
      (at 10.0 5.0 1.2).x ]

  x-want : List Real
  x-want = [480.0, 1280.0, 80.0, 2080.0]

 Height first: a point at the eye is on the horizon, one above it is higher up
 the screen -- a SMALLER y -- and one below is lower.

  y-got : List Real
  y-got =
    [ (at 0.0 10.0 1.2).y,
      (at 0.0 10.0 11.2).y,
      (at 0.0 10.0 6.2).y,
      (at 0.0 10.0 0.2).y,
      (at 0.0 4.0 3.2).y ]

  y-want : List Real
  y-want = [300.0, -500.0, -100.0, 380.0, -100.0]

 Distance is the divide, so the same world offset shrinks as it recedes: at
 twice the forward, half the displacement.

  depth-got : List Real
  depth-got =
    [ (at 4.0 4.0 1.2).x, (at 4.0 8.0 1.2).x, (at 4.0 16.0 1.2).x,
      (at 0.0 4.0 5.2).y, (at 0.0 8.0 5.2).y, (at 0.0 16.0 5.2).y ]

  depth-want : List Real
  depth-want = [1280.0, 880.0, 680.0, -500.0, -100.0, 100.0]

  fixed-got : List Real
  fixed-got = [camera-h, eye-h]

  fixed-want : List Real
  fixed-want = [600.0, 1.2]

Section: The List Form

 `project` in a loop, in order, and an empty list projects to nothing.

  three : List Vec3
  three =
    [ Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = 10.0, height = 1.2 },
      Vec3 { right = 10.0, forward = 10.0, height = 1.2 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = 10.0, height = 11.2 } ]

  all-got : List Real
  all-got =
    let sp = project-all three 800.0 960.0 0
    in [ real-from-int (list-length sp),
         (list-at sp 0).x, (list-at sp 1).x, (list-at sp 2).y,
         real-from-int (list-length (project-all [] 800.0 960.0 0)) ]

  all-want : List Real
  all-want = [3.0, 480.0, 1280.0, -500.0, 0.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cm-x    " x-got x-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cm-y    " y-got y-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cm-depth" depth-got depth-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cm-fixed" fixed-got fixed-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cm-all  " all-got all-want 0.0)
  end
